782:      "finding": "`(\"module\", TokenKind::Module)` is at src/lexer.rs:43 and `(\"export\", TokenKind::Export)` at src/lexer.rs:45, and SPEC.md:618-648 plus docs/GRAMMAR.md:143-157 document a full module system. Neither token has an arm in the statement dispatch at src/parser.rs:420 (`grep -c 'TokenKind::Module\\|TokenKind::Export' src/parser.rs` is 0 each): `module M ... end` fails with `ParserError: Unexpected token Module`. `import` IS handled (src/parser.rs:465) but the `as` alias it parses at src/parser.rs:501 is never bound, so `import json as J` then `J.parse(\"{}\")` fails with `AnalyzerError: Unknown variable 'as'` and `Unknown variable 'J'`. Nothing in the repo exercises a module declaration: neither modules/MathUtils.rb nor modules/SuiteKit.rb contains the word `module`, so the `examples/` sweep in rbops/verify.sh:225 has never run one.",
1125:      "finding": "`Expr::InterpolatedText(Vec<Expr>)` is declared at src/parser.rs:56 and is handled by four consumers — src/analyzer.rs:437, src/interpreter.rs:1515, src/formatter.rs:734 and src/linter.rs:381 — but the parser has ZERO construction sites: `grep -n 'InterpolatedText' src/parser.rs` prints exactly one line, the declaration at :56. The text-literal arm at src/parser.rs:2023-2026 does `TokenKind::Text(s) => { self.advance(); Ok(Expr::Text(s.clone())) }`, passing the braces through verbatim, so all four consumers are dead code. Verified against the built binary: `set name to \"Bob\"` then `say \"Hello, {name}!\"` prints `Hello, {name}!` and exits 0 — the NAME, not the value. SPEC.md:293-298 (`### String Interpolation`, `set greeting to \"Hello, {name}!\"`, `set message to \"Value: {x + y}\"`) and SPEC.md:526 document the form as working; AGENTS.md section 2 lists \"Trailing-comma and `{interp}` string syntax\" as a LANGUAGE INVARIANT that must not silently change, so the invariant is violated today, not merely missing. `rb run examples/hello.rb` prints `Hello, {name}!` as its third line, and the example sweep in rbops/verify.sh runs it and only checks the exit code. A GREEN test pins the broken behaviour as correct: tests/test_text.rb:37 `test \"text: braces are ordinary characters in a text literal\"` asserts `expect \"Hello, {name}!\" to be \"Hello, {name}!\"` at :41 and `expect length(\"Hello, {name}!\") to be 14` at :42, with a comment at :38 admitting the VM does not substitute. This defect was found and recorded as severity blocker by THREE separate phases — phases/phase-003/FINDINGS.md:44-64, phases/phase-008/FINDINGS.md:11-34 and phases/phase-016/FINDINGS.md:77 — and no phase was ever written for it, because FINDINGS.md is a report and the manifest is the queue.",
1966:        "the file lives at the repository root and NOT under `.opencode/agent/`, which rbops/verify.sh:127 treats as a forbidden path",
2891:      "finding": "`cargo doc --no-deps` reports 34 warnings on this tree, measured this audit, and no gate in this project can see a single one. One is unresolved: src/runtime.rs:3 links `[`crate::vm`]` and rustc answers 'no item named `vm` in module `redblue`' — the tree-walker is src/interpreter.rs and there is no src/vm.rs. src/bytecode/vm.rs:3 and src/runtime.rs:560 make the same link, and README.md:292 repeats the mistake in prose, listing `vm.rs  # Tree-walking interpreter` under Project Structure for a file that does not exist. The other 33 are `private_intra_doc_links`: public documentation pointing at private items — `crate::runtime` and `BytecodeVm::call_depth` and `loop_sites` from src/bytecode/vm.rs:3, `crate::interpreter::Vm::call_method` from src/stdlib.rs:148, `run_lock` from src/wasm.rs:643, `STAGE2` from src/lib.rs. None is reachable: the four gates in AGENTS.md § 3.4 are fmt, clippy, test and verify.sh, clippy does not run rustdoc lints, and `grep -rn 'cargo doc' .github/workflows/ rbops/*.sh` returns nothing.",
2930:      "title": "Make every FAIL in verify.sh print the remedy next to it",
2935:      "finding": "A gate check that states a RULE but not a REMEDY is not a gate, it is a wall. Phase-088 failed four times in about 24 minutes on one line: 'FAIL no test asserts a failure is produced - mandatory'. The agent received that line every time - the gateout injection added on 2026-10-16 puts the verbatim FAIL into the retry context and it appears in every Dispatch log - and it shipped correct work reporting '1774 passed, 0 failed', which is TRUE and is not what the check measures. The check asks whether any test asserts that something FAILS; a suite can be entirely green and satisfy nothing there. Phase-082 spent three attempts and about 4.5 hours rediscovering the same shape for edge_*. Both needed a human-written RETRY-NOTE.md, which is a note that should not have been necessary: verify.sh already does this correctly for one check. The missing-REPORT.md branch explains itself in five lines naming the exact file and section, and phase-019 stopped failing that check immediately. Every other check emits a bare verdict. The remedy text is known and static - it is the same advice a human writes in a retry note every time.",
2936:      "goal": "Give every FAIL in verify.sh the remedy beside it, the way the missing-REPORT.md check already does. The remedy must be specific and actionable: name the file to change, the construct to use, and the reason the obvious alternative does not satisfy the check. Implement as a lookup table in verify.sh so the text lives in one place and every check can call it; a remedy printed from a shared helper cannot drift from the check it explains. Do not weaken, relax or skip any check. Do not change any check's PASS/FAIL logic - this phase changes only what is printed after a check has already failed. Declare manual: it changes rbops/, which the bot token cannot push.",
2938:        "every FAIL emitted by verify.sh is followed by at least one indented remedy line saying what to change, and a test asserts no FAIL line is emitted bare",
2943:        "no PASS/FAIL decision changes as a result of this phase - asserted by running verify.sh against a phase that passes and one that fails, before and after, and comparing the verdicts",
